# A setoid is a carrier paired with an equivalence relation on it. It is the constructive stand-in for a quotient. Rather
# than forming a new type whose elements are equivalence classes, a setoid keeps the original elements and carries the
# relation that says when two of them count as the same. The three laws of an equivalence (reflexive, symmetric,
# transitive) are exactly the laws that make such an identification coherent, and a function out of a setoid is allowed
# to depend only through the relation, the congruence condition. This file constructs a concrete setoid, proves its
# three laws and a respecting function by the kernel, states the quotient and its universal property, and refutes a
# relation that fails transitivity.
#
# The relation is realized as the KERNEL OF A FUNCTION: pick a fingerprint map `double` on the carrier and declare two
# elements related when they share a fingerprint, `x ~ y` iff `double x = double y`. Every kernel relation is an
# equivalence for free, since equality of fingerprints is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. The proofs below are
# universal (over every element), discharged by computation and by the hypothesis-discharge path that assumes each
# antecedent on its branch.

form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# The fingerprint. `double n` counts each step of n twice, so it separates the naturals into the same blocks equality
# does (it is injective here), but in general a fingerprint collapses distinct inputs to one block. The relation reads
# off this map.
task double
  take n, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                call double
                  read prior

# REFLEXIVE: every element is related to itself, double a = double a. The first setoid law, immediate by reflexivity of
# equality.
rule relation-is-reflexive
  seat a, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read a
  calm hold

# SYMMETRIC: if a is related to b then b is related to a. From double a = double b, conclude double b = double a. Proved
# by assuming the antecedent on the branch and rewriting it into the goal.
rule relation-is-symmetric
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        read b
      call double
        read a
  calm hold

# TRANSITIVE: if a is related to b and b is related to c then a is related to c. From double a = double b and
# double b = double c, conclude double a = double c. The two antecedents chain through the shared middle fingerprint.
# With the two laws above this proves the relation is an EQUIVALENCE, so the carrier is a genuine setoid.
rule relation-is-transitive
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  have h1
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  have h2
    call is-equal
      call double
        read b
      call double
        read c
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read c
  calm hold

# CONGRUENCE: a function RESPECTS the relation when it sends related inputs to related outputs, a ~ b implies f a ~ f b.
# This is the condition for a function to descend to the quotient. The successor respects the relation: from
# double a = double b conclude double (succ a) = double (succ b), since double of a successor is two more than double,
# and adding the same amount to equal fingerprints keeps them equal. A respecting function is well-defined on classes.
rule successor-respects-relation
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
      call double
        make succ
          bind prior
            read b
  calm hold

# THE QUOTIENT. The quotient of a setoid is the type of its equivalence classes, one class per block of related
# elements. A class is named by any of its members, and two names pick the same class exactly when the members are
# related. The constructive content of the quotient is therefore already present in the setoid: the class data is the
# carrier, and class equality IS the relation. The two laws below pin this down.

# EQUALITY ON CLASSES IS THE RELATION, one direction: related members name the same class. Here a class is named by its
# fingerprint, so members that are related (equal fingerprints) name one class. This is reflexivity of equality on the
# fingerprint, the witness that the relation refines to class equality.
rule class-of-related-is-one
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  calm hold

# THE UNIVERSAL PROPERTY: a function that respects the relation DESCENDS to the quotient, meaning its value depends only
# on the class, not on the chosen member. Concretely, for the respecting map `double` itself, related members give equal
# results, so the induced map on classes is well-defined and the descended map agrees with the original on every member.
# This is the defining property of the quotient: respecting functions factor uniquely through it.
rule respecting-function-descends
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call double
        read a
      call double
        read b
  calm hold

# A NON-EQUIVALENCE, refuted. A relation that holds only between neighbors fails transitivity and so is NOT an
# equivalence, so it has no well-defined quotient. Take three colors in a line and call two of them near when they are
# equal or adjacent. Then red is near green and green is near blue, but red is NOT near blue, the transitive step that an
# equivalence would force. The verdict type carries the two distinct answers the kernel separates.
form color
  case red
  case green
  case blue

form verdict
  case yes
  case no

task near
  take x, like color
  take y, like color
  like verdict
  fork case, read x
    case red
      fork case, read y
        case red
          send back
            make yes
        case green
          send back
            make yes
        case blue
          send back
            make no
    case green
      send back
        make yes
    case blue
      fork case, read y
        case red
          send back
            make no
        case green
          send back
            make yes
        case blue
          send back
            make yes

# red is near green.
rule red-is-near-green
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call near
        make red
        make green
      make yes
  calm hold

# green is near blue.
rule green-is-near-blue
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call near
        make green
        make blue
      make yes
  calm hold

# But red is NOT near blue, so `near` is not transitive and not an equivalence. The two ends of the line are unrelated
# even though each is related to the middle. Refuted by computation: the verdict is `no`, distinct from `yes`.
rule red-is-not-near-blue
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call near
        make red
        make blue
      make yes
  calm miss
